        "math_academy_id": "question-008",
        "problem": "Let $A$ and $B$ be sets. Prove that \n\n$$\n\\overset{\u2015}{A\\cup B}=\\overset{\u2015}{A}\\cap \\overset{\u2015}{B}.\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "To prove a set equality using biconditionals, we must show an element belongs to one set if and only if it belongs to the other.\n\nIn this case, we're required to prove De Morgan's Law for disjunctions:\n\n$$\n\\overset{\u2015}{A\\cup B}=\\overset{\u2015}{A}\\cap \\overset{\u2015}{B}\n$$\n\nSince we're proving this statement using a sequence of biconditionals, we begin our proof as follows:\n\nFor any element $x,$ the sequence of logical equivalences unfolds as follows:\n\nFirst, we assume $x\\in \\overset{\u2015}{A\\cup B}$ and apply the definition of the set complement:\n\nTo belong to $\\overset{\u2015}{A\\cup B}$ is synonymous with not belonging to $A\\cup B:$\n\n$$\nx\\in \\overset{\u2015}{A\\cup B}\\,\u21d4\\,x\\notin A\\cup B\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of negation and the definition of union:\n\nNot being a member of the union $A\\cup B$ is equivalent to not satisfying the conditions $x\\in A$ or $x\\in B$ individually: \n\n$$\nx\\notin A\\cup B\\,\u21d4\\,\u00ac(x\\in A\u2228x\\in B)\n$$\n\nNext, we apply De Morgan's law for disjunctions: $\u00ac(P\u2228Q)\u2261\u00acP\u2227\u00acQ:$\n\nThe negation of the conditions individually is the same as the conjunction of their negations by De Morgan's Law for disjunction:\n\n$$\n\u00ac(x\\in A\u2228x\\in B)\\,\u21d4\\,\u00ac(x\\in A)\u2227\u00ac(x\\in B)\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of negation gives:\n\nThe conjunction of the negations corresponds to not belonging to $A$ and not belonging to $B:$\n\n$$\n\u00ac(x\\in A)\u2227\u00ac(x\\in B)\\,\u21d4\\,(x\\notin A)\u2227(x\\notin B)\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the definition of the set complement and intersection gives:\n\nConsequently, the combined condition is satisfied if and only if $x$ belongs to the intersections of the complements, sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x\\notin A)\u2227(x\\notin B)\\, & \u21d4\\,(x\\in \\overset{\u2015}{A})\u2227(x\\in \\overset{\u2015}{B}) \\\\  & \u21d4\\,x\\in \\overset{\u2015}{A}\\cap \\overset{\u2015}{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing the sequence of equivalent statements, we've shown that an element belongs to one set if and only if it belongs to the other. Thus, our proof is complete, and we write down our conclusion.\n\nThus, it follows that for any element $x,$ membership in $\\overset{\u2015}{A\\cup B}$ is equivalent to membership in $\\overset{\u2015}{A}\\cap \\overset{\u2015}{B},$ leading to the conclusion:\n\n$$\n\\overset{\u2015}{A\\cup B}=\\overset{\u2015}{A}\\cap \\overset{\u2015}{B}\n$$\n\nPROOF\n\nFor any element $x,$ the sequence of logical equivalences unfolds as follows:\n\nTo belong to $\\overset{\u2015}{A\\cup B}$ is equivalent to not belonging to $A\\cup B$:\n\n$$\nx\\in \\overset{\u2015}{A\\cup B}\\,\u21d4\\,x\\notin A\\cup B.\n$$\n\nNot being a member of the union $A\\cup B$ is equivalent to not satisfying the conditions $x\\in A$ or $x\\in B$ individually:\n\n$$\nx\\notin A\\cup B\\,\u21d4\\,\u00ac(x\\in A\u2228x\\in B)\n$$\n\nThe negation of the conditions individually  is the same as the conjunction of their respective  negations by De Morgan's Law for disjunction:\n\n$$\n\u00ac(x\\in A\u2228x\\in B)\\,\u21d4\\,\u00ac(x\\in A)\u2227\u00ac(x\\in B)\n$$\n\nThe conjunction of the negations corresponds to not belonging to $A$ and not belonging to $B$:\n\n$$\n\u00ac(x\\in A)\u2227\u00ac(x\\in B)\\,\u21d4\\,(x\\notin A)\u2227(x\\notin B)\n$$\n\nConsequently, the combined condition is satisfied if and only if $x$ belongs to the intersection of the complements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x\\notin A)\u2227(x\\notin B)\\, & \u21d4\\,(x\\in \\overset{\u2015}{A})\u2227(x\\in \\overset{\u2015}{B}) \\\\  & \u21d4\\,x\\in \\overset{\u2015}{A}\\cap \\overset{\u2015}{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, it follows that for any element $x,$ membership in $\\overset{\u2015}{A\\cup B}$ is equivalent to membership in $\\overset{\u2015}{A}\\cap \\overset{\u2015}{B}$, leading to the conclusion:\n\n$$\n\\overset{\u2015}{A\\cup B}=\\overset{\u2015}{A}\\cap \\overset{\u2015}{B}\n$$",
